= Turing completeness — Technical =

== Simulation criterion ==
A computational formalism F is commonly called Turing complete when there exists an effective encoding enabling F to simulate arbitrary Turing-machine computations.

Schematically:

<pre>
for each Turing machine M,
encode(M,x) in F reproduces M(x)
</pre>

under idealized resource assumptions.

== Universal interpreters ==
A language can establish universality by implementing a universal machine, lambda-calculus evaluator, register machine, tag system, or another known Turing-equivalent formalism.

== Restrictions ==
Total functional languages, finite-state languages, some query languages, proof assistants' terminating cores, and smart-contract DSLs can intentionally restrict expressiveness to obtain termination or analyzability properties.

== Undecidability consequences ==
For sufficiently general semantic properties of Turing-complete programs, Rice-style undecidability results apply.

Turing completeness therefore increases expressive generality while limiting complete static analysis.

== Terminology ==
“Turing complete” for recursively enumerable sets under Turing reducibility is a distinct recursion-theory usage.

